## Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), synthesized by coordinationdirected assembly of metal ions and organic molecules, are emerging as a class of functional materials for diverse applications [1-14]. Especially, MOFs-mediated delivery of nucleic acids, including small interfering RNA and single-stranded antisense oligonucleotides (AsO), has been gaining momentum for gene interference therapy [15-2l]. Encapsulation of nucleic acids in nanoscale MOFs (nMOFs) has been achieved through a biomimetic mineralization method [18-25]. However, this onestep approach is limited to the using of MOFs that are synthesized in aqueous solution (e.g., ZIF-type MOFs). Since many functional MOFs (e.g., PCN-type MOFs) are synthesized in organic solvents, much effort has been devoted to the post synthetic attachment of DNA on MOFs for expanding their applications [26-31]. For example, Mirkin and co-workers developed strategies for the attachment of terminal-functionalized DNA on MOFs through covalent or coordination-mediated conjugation [26-29]. Although these systems show great promise for diverse applications, these approaches are often limited by the synthetic DNA modifications. Recently, it was demonstrated that unmodified DNA could be directly loaded on the surface of MOFs through the coordination interactions between unsaturated metal sites exposed on MOFs and DNA backbone phosphate, which allows for drug delivery and tumor treatment [32-37]. However, these systems suffer from relatively low DNA loading content on nMOFs due to the monolayer loading mechanism. Moreover, the using of such nanostructures for gene delivery is often hampered by the limited endosomal escape ability. Therefore, development of simple methods for efficient and controlled integration of nucleic acids with nMOFs for gene delivery is still a challenge.
Previously, we reported the synthesis of metal-DNA hybrid nanoparticles through coordination-mediated assembly of DNA molecules and metal ions [38]. Thus motivated, here we develop an extremely simple approach for the efficient and controlled loading of DNA molecules on nMOFs via the self-assembly of metal-DNA coordination polymers on nMOFs (termed MOF@MD, Fig.l(a)). In comparison with the methods mentioned above, our approach allows for ultrahigh DNA loading content on nMOFs with precisely adjustable ratios via tuning of the metal-DNA shell thickness. Furthermore, by employing porphyrinic nMOFs (PCN-224) as the core, and Bcl-2 antisense ASO [39-42] and small molecular drug (doxorubicin (Dox)） as assembled shell, core-shell heterostructures (named MOF@MDD) were prepared to achieve combinational tumor treatment (Fig. 1(b)). The porphyrinic nMOF core of MOF $@$ MD-D could generate large quantity of reactive oxygen species (ROS) upon light irradiation, which allows to boost the endosomal escape of the system for an enhanced gene silencing efcacy. Ultimately, the hierarchical nanostructures render augmented anticancer capacity through the combination of highly efficient gene therapy and photodynamic therapy (PDT) with chemotherapy.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of PCN-224
The PCN-224 nMOFs were synthesized according to the previous method [43].Briefly, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}$ $(20\mathrm{mg})$ ， $\mathrm{ZrOCl_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ 0 $\mathrm{\dot{60}m g)}$ and benzoic acid ( $\mathrm{560\mg)}$ were dissolved in N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) $\mathrm{20~mL}$ . After sonication for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ 。the mixture was heated at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ with stirring. The as-synthesized PCN-224 were obtained by centrifugation $(13,000\ \mathrm{rpm},20\ \mathrm{min})$ . Then the products were washed 3 times with fresh DMF and re-dispersed in $10\mathrm{mL}$ DMF for future use.
## 2.2 Synthesis of MOF@MD
The nMOFs were washed 5 times with Millipore water and redispersed in Millipore water. In a typical synthesis of $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{MOF@MD}.}\end{array}$ $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ 1 $(20\mathrm{\mM})$ was added to a centrifuge tube containing $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ASO（ $\mathrm{~\small~{~\cdot~}~m M})$ ， $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ nMOFs and $371\ \upmu\mathrm{L}$ Millipore water. The mixture was heated at $95~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ after a vigorous vortex. Then the resulting $\mathbf{MOF@MD}$ were obtained by centrifugation ( $(13,000~\mathrm{rpm}$ 。 $10\mathrm{min}$ ） and re-dispersed in Millipore water. To obtain the $\mathrm{MOF@MD}$ with different shell thicknesses, the $\mathrm{ASO/Fe^{\mathrm{\pi}}}$ molar ratio was set to the same, and the amount of ASO was varied: $1\#$ (5 nmol ASO), $2\#$ (10 nmol ASO),3# (20 nmol ASO). To evaluate the ASO-loading efficiency of ${\mathrm{MOF@MD}}.$ the supernatant and washing solution of the system were colected. The unloaded ASO can be determined by ultraviolet-visible (UV-Vis） spectrometry according to the extinction coefficients.
## 2.3 Quantification of DNA strands on nMOF
The zirconium content of the MOF $@$ MDs was determined by inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) on a NexION 30oX instrument (Perkinelmer). Then, the particle concentration of nMOF was calculated based on the previously reported method [43].After assembly of DNA on nMOF,the nanoparticles were centrifuged and washed. All the supernatants were collected for calculating the amount of unloaded DNA. The
DNA concentrations were determined by UV-Vis spectrometry at $260\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Finally, the average number of DNA strands per nMOF was calculated by taking the ratio of loaded DNA amount (in moles) to the particle concentration of nMOF (in moles).
## 2.4 Synthesis of MOF@MD-D
$20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ 0 $\mathrm{\Omega}20\ \mathrm{mM})$ and $5~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of Dox( $\mathrm{{(10~mM)}}$ were added to a centrifuge tube containing $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of ASO $\left(1\mathrm{mM}\right)$ ,10 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of nMOFs and $366~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Millipore water. The mixture was heated at $95~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ after a vigorous vortex. Finally, the resulting $\mathrm{MOF@MD-D}$ were obtained by centrifugation (13,000 rpm, $10\mathrm{min}$ ）and re-dispersed in Millipore water.
## 2.5 Assessment of ROS generation in test tube
The mixture of singlet oxygen sensor green (SOsG) stock solution $(5\ \mathrm{mM},\ 5\ \upmu\mathrm{L})$ and $\mathrm{MOF@MD-D}$ solution (phosphate buffered saline (PBS), $1\mathrm{mL}$ ）were kept in the dark or irradiated by $640\mathrm{nm}$ light $\mathrm{(0.1~W/cm^{2})}$ for various periods of time. The recovered SOSG fluorescence under $504~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation was used to evaluate the generation of ROS.
## 2.6 Cellular uptake assay
MCF-7 cells were incubated at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ inside a glass bottom dish (Cellvis） for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ 、 Then fresh medium containing free ASO or $\mathrm{MOF@MD-D}$ (ASO was labeled with $\mathrm{Cy}5.5,$ ASO equivalent to $430\mathrm{nM})$ was added. After incubation for different times, the cells were washed with PBS. Then the cells were stained with Hoechst 33342 and LysoTracker Green, and imaged by a Zeiss 710 single photon confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM).
## 2.7 In vitro ROS evaluation
The ROS generation produced by MOF $@$ MD-D with or without 640 nm lightirradiation wasevaluatedvia2,7- dichlorodihydrofluorescein diacetate (DCFH-DA). Typically, MCF-7 cells were incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ inside a glass bottom dish. Next, fresh medium containing $\mathrm{MOF@MD-D}$ (ASO equivalent to $860\mathrm{nM},$ ）was added.The cells were washed with PBS after $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ incubation. Fresh culture medium containing $20~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ DCFH-DA was added into the cells for another $20~\mathrm{\min}$ incubation. Then the cells were washed with PBS three times, and kept in the dark or irradiated with a $640~\mathrm{nm}$ light $(0.1\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ for $20~\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the cells were stained with Hoechst 33342 and imaged by CLSM.
## 2.8 Western blotting
MCF-7 cells were incubated with complete Dulbecco's modified Eagle's medium (DMEM) culture medium inside a 6-well plate (NEST Biotechnology). After $^{12\ \mathrm{h},}$ cells were incubated with $\mathbf{MOF@MD}$ 。or $\mathrm{MOF@MnD}$ for another $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ Then cells were washed with PBS and kept in the dark or irradiated with a $640\mathrm{nm}$ light $(0.1\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ .Untreated cells were used as control. The cellular proteins were extracted using the nuclear and cytoplasmic protein extraction kit according to the manufacturer's instructions. $20~\upmu\mathrm{L/lane}$ of proteins were separated by sodium dodecyl sulfate-poly-acrylamide gels (SDS-PAGE) and electro transferred to $0.22\textrm{~\textmu m}$ polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) membrane. $5\%$ (w/v) nonfat milk in TBST (Tris buffered saline supplemented with $0.1\%$ $\left(\mathrm{v/v}\right)$ Tween-20) was used to block the PVDF membranes. Then, the PVDF membranes were incubated with anti-Bcl-2 antibody (mouse monoclonal antibody, 1:1000 dilution, Solarbio, K200018M)， followed by horseradish peroxidase (HRP)-conjugated secondary antibodies (Abbkine, A21010, l:5000 dilution). Protein bands were obtained by a ChemiDoc $\mathrm{XRS+}$ with Image Lab software (Bio-Rad, USA).
## 2.9 Cellular cytotoxicity assay
MCF-7 cells were cultured overnight inside a 96-well microplate $\mathrm{2\times10^{4}}$ cells/well). Fresh medium containing $\mathrm{MOF@MnD-D}$ or $\mathrm{MOF@MD-D}$ (ASO equivalent to $860\ \mathrm{\nM})$ was added and incubated for $4\mathrm{h}.$ Then the cells were cultured with fresh medium, and kept in the dark or irradiated with a $640\mathrm{nm}$ light $(0.1\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ . After another $48\mathrm{{h}}$ incubation, $10\%$ Cell Counting Kit8 (CCK-8)was added to each well for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ The absorbance at a wavelength of $450\mathrm{nm}$ was measured by a microplate reader.
## 2.10 Cell apoptosis assay
MCF-7 cells were cultured inside a 6-well plate (NEST Biotechnology） $(2\times10^{5}$ cells/well） for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Fresh cell culture medium containing $\mathrm{MOF@MnD-D}$ or MOF@MD-D (ASO equivalent to $860~\mathrm{nM}$ ）wasadded.After $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ incubation, the cells were cultured with fresh medium, and kept in the dark or irradiated with a $640\ \mathrm{nm}$ light $(0.1\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ .After $48\mathrm{{h}}$ incubation, cells were stained with Annexin V and propidium iodide (PI) for flow cytometric analysis.
## 2.11 In vivo tumor therapy
Six groups( $\mathit{\Pi}_{\mathcal{n}}=\mathit{\Pi}5\mathit{\Pi}_{\mathcal{n}}$ ）of mice, which marked as PBS, PBS $(+),$ $\mathrm{MOF@MD-D}$ ， $\mathrm{MOF@MD-D}$ $\left(+\right)$ ， $\mathrm{MOF@MnD-D}$ and $\mathrm{MOF@MnD-D\(}$ $\left(+\right)$ ,were divided randomly for further treatment. The tumor-bearing mice were tail-vein injected with $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of MOF $@$ MnD-D, MOF@MD-D (PCN-224 dose of $5~\mathrm{mg/kg})$ and PBS, respectively. After $6\mathrm{{h}}$ injection, the $640~\mathrm{nm}$ light irradiation (0.1 W/cm, 20 min) was conducted. The body weights and tumor sizes were recorded every 2 days for l4 days. The tumor volume was calculated as follows (Eq.(1)):
$$
\mathrm{Volume}=\mathrm{Width}^{2}\times\frac{\mathrm{Length}}{2}
$$
The tumor slices were stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E).
## 2.12 Blood biochemical analysis
Two groups $(n~=~5)$ ） of mice were divided randomly and intravenously injected with PBS or MOF $@.$ MD-D (PCN-224 dose of $5\mathrm{mg/kg)}$ . The serum of mice was extracted and analyzed using automatic biochemical analyzer (Hitachi-7l00, Japan） after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ injection.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Synthesis and characterization of MOF@MD
Importantly, by simply increasing the added amounts of DNA and metal ions during the synthesis (the molar ratio of $\mathrm{{DNA}/\mathrm{{Fe}^{\mathrm{{I}}}}}$ was set to the same), the loading content of DNA on the surface of a single nMOF could be tailored via the control of the thickness of the assembled Fe-DNA shell. As shown in Figs. 2(e)-2(h), a series of core-shell structured $\mathbf{MOF@MD}$ with shell thickness ranging from approximately 14 to $46\ \mathrm{nm}$ were obtained. As a result, average number of DNA strands on a single nMOF increased from $\sim4.4\times10^{4}$ to $\sim2.2\times10^{5}$ strands (Fig.2(i)), while the loading efficiency of DNA remained high (Fig.2(j). In contrast, the traditional method enabled lower DNA loading content ( $(\sim4.8\times$ $10^{3}$ strands per nMOF), indicating that the DNA loading capacity of $\mathbf{MOF@MD}$ is considerably higher than the traditional method that relies on the absorption of DNA on nMOFs [32]. This is mainly attributed to the intrinsic coordination-driven selfassembly of multiple layers of DNA on the surface of nMOFs.
We then monitored the growth process by characterizing the products at different time intervals by TEM. It displayed that metal-DNA coordination polymers grow on the surface of nMOFs at an early reaction time (within $10~\mathrm{min}$ ）(Fig.S7 in the ESM).It has been established that DNA could assemble with $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{\scriptscriptstyleII}}}$ ions to yield coordination NPs due to their metal-ion binding sites in phosphate groups and nucleobases [38]. Since DNA can supply plenty of phosphate groups to coordinate with the coordinativelyunsaturated metal sites of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters on the nMOFs surface,we speculate that attachment of DNA on the nMOFs surface promotes the heterogeneous growth of Fe-DNA coordination shell. As a control, we performed the synthesis in the absence of $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{\scriptscriptstyleII}}}$ ions. The results showed that no core-shell structure was observed (Fig.S8 in the ESM), confirming the critical role of FeDNA coordination for shell growth. To prove the generality of our strategy, rod-shaped PCN-222 nMOFs were used for heterostructure fabrication. As expected, a dense shell of Fe-DNA could be formed uniformly on PCN-222 nMOFs (Fig.S9 in the ESM), demonstrating the universality of this approach.
## 3.2 Photochemistry properties of MOF@MD-D
Next, we explored the use of the heterostructures for combinational tumor therapy. A Bcl-2 ASO anda chemotherapeutic drug (Dox) were used for the co-assembly with $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{\scriptscriptstyleII}}}$ ions in the presence of PCN-224 nMOFs, resulting in the core-shell heterostructures (MOF@MD-D, Figs. 3(a)-3(c)). HAADF-STEM-EDS element mapping (Fig.3(d)) and the EDS line scan(Fig.3(e)) furtherconfirmedthecore-shell heterostructures of MOF@MD-D. TEM images showed that both MOF@MD and MOF@MD-D were stable in water for more than $12\mathrm{{h}}$ (Fig. S10 in the ESM). The introduction of Dox improved the stability of the heterostructures in PBS (Fig.S1l in the ESM). We then evaluated the singlet oxygen $\left({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ generation of MOF $@$ MDD through a standard SOSG assay. As shown in Figs. 3(f) and $3(\mathrm{g)}$ P under the irradiation of a $640~\mathrm{nm}$ light, the fluorescence intensity of SOSG in MOF $@$ MD-D solution increased in an irradiation dose-dependent manner. By contrast, the SOsG fluorescence remained unchanged without light irradiation, confirming that the photodynamic property of PCN-224 nMOFs was retained after the formation of the heterostructures.
We then performed confocal imaging analysis to delineate the cellular internalization capability of MOF@MD-D (ASO was labeled with Cy5.5). The fluorescence signal increased steadily over the incubation time, indicative of time-dependent cellular uptake of MOF $@$ MD-D (Fig. S12 in the ESM). Notably, the cells incubated with MOF $@$ MD-D exhibited an obvious fluorescence signal than those treated with free ASO (Fig. $3(\mathrm{h})\dot{}$ ), indicating that the system enables a significantly enhanced intracellular delivery of ASO. The light-induced generation of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in living cells was also confirmed by using DCFH-DA as an intracellular probe (Fig. 3(i)).A significant increase of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ signal was observed when MCF7 cells were treated with MOF@MD-D following light irradiation, demonstrating the effective ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation of MOF $@$ MD-D in living cells.
## 3.3 Boosted endosomal escape efficiency
To track the MOF $@$ MD-D at subcellular level following their uptake, the treated MCF-7 cells were stained with LysoTracker Green DND-26. As shown in Fig.3(j), after incubation of MOF@MD-D, the red fluorescence of ASO overlaps obviously with the green fluorescence of LysoTracker (with Pearson's coefficient of 0.65). In contrast, the overlap between the fluorescence signals of ASO and LysoTracker is significantly reduced (with Pearson's coefficient of 0.36) when the $\mathbf{MOF@MD}$ 1 D-treated cells are irradiated with a $640\ \mathrm{nm}$ light, indicating an enhanced endosomal escape efficiency. This could be attributed to the cytotoxic ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ mediated rupture of the endo/lysosome membranes via massive peroxidation of the membrane lipids [44].
## 3.4 In vitro cytotoxic evaluation
We next assess therapeutic efficacy of the heterostructures in vitro. As illustrated in Fig. 4(a), a mild therapeutic activity was observed for $\mathrm{MOF@MD-D}$ while higher cell cytotoxicity was seen for the $\mathrm{MOF@MD-D}$ plus light irradiation group due to the combination of gene therapy and PDT with chemotherapy. The treatment with $\mathrm{MOF@MnD-D}$ (synthesized with a non-therapeutic DNA） plus light irradiation resulted in a relatively lower effects on cell viability, attributing to its lack of gene therapy. The Annexin V and PI double staining analysis showed that the apoptosis/necrosis rate of the MOF $@$ MD-D $\left(+\right)$ group was significantly increased to $82.2\%$ compared to $27.7\%$ for the $\mathrm{MOF@MD-D}$ group (Fig. 4(b)). To further confirm the gene regulation effect of the heterostructures, we incubated MCF-7 cells with $\mathbf{MOF@MD}$ and then assayed bcl-2 gene expression by western blot analysis. MCF7 cells without any treatment were used as a negative control. As shown in Fig.4(c) and Fig.Si3 in the ESM, light irradiation of the treated cells significantly reduced Bcl-2 protein expression level compared to the group without irradiation, indicating that the photodynamic effects-boosted endosomal escape enhanced the gene silencing performance of ASO. Of note, the Bcl-2 protein maintains a normal level for the MOF@MnD-treated cells, regardless of light irradiation or not. Together, these data suggest that the heterostructures can exert a prominent anti-cancer effect by the combinational therapy.
## 3.5In vivo anti-tumor therapy
To test the antitumor activity of the system in vivo, tumor-bearing BALB/c mice were intravenously injected with PBS, MOF@MnDD orMOF $@$ MD-D.At $6\textup{h}$ post-injection, the mice were either kept in the dark or irradiated with $640\mathrm{nm}$ light at the tumor sites.
The weights and tumor volumes of mice were monitored every other day after different treatments. As shown in Fig. 5(a), rapid tumor growth was observed for the groups of PBS and PBS $\left(+\right)$ Treatment with MOF@MnD-D, MOF@MnD-D $\left(+\right)$ or MOF $@$ MD-D had moderate tumor inhibition, while MOF@MDD $\left(+\right)$ resulted in the highest anti-tumor effect due to the combination of PDT with gene-/chemo-therapy. The tumors from all groups were harvested after treatments, the tumor volumes (Fig. 5(b)) and tumor weights (Fig. 5(c)) were conformed to their growth curves. Meanwhile, the body weights of all groups fluctuated within a rational scope, and there was no significant loss during the experimental period (Fig. 5(d)). H&E staining revealed the highest level of apoptotic/necrotic cells in the MOF@MD-D $\left(+\right)$ group among all groups (Fig.5(e)). Moreover, blood biochemical analysis showed no obvious liver, and kidney toxicity or inflammation in the treated mice, indicating the high biocompatibility of the heterostructures (Fig. Sl4 in the ESM).
## 4 Conclusions
In conclusion, we have developed a facile and general strategy for integration of DNA with nMOFs via the metal-DNA coordination chemistry. With this strategy, hierarchical metal-organic@metalDNA nanostructures were engineered, leading to the loading of DNA molecules on the surface of nMOFs with an ultra-high loading capacity and precise controllability. The approach allows to integrate gene therapy and chemotherapy with photodynamic therapy for combinational tumor treatment via the co-assembly strategy. Furthermore, the heterostructures allow for lighttriggered endosomal escape and thus enhanced gene silencing efficacy. This work highlights the potential for coordinationdriven assembly of hierarchical nanostructures in ways that are simple, versatile, and generalizable to diverse organic and biomolecularspecies,with wide applications in nanobiotechnology and programmable drug delivery.
